<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<?xml-model href="https://www.tei-c.org/Vault/P5/4.12.0/xml/tei/custom/schema/relaxng/tei_all.rng" type="application/xml" schematypens="http://relaxng.org/ns/structure/1.0"?>
<TEI xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0" xml:lang="en">
  <teiHeader>
    <fileDesc>
      <titleStmt>
        <title>Fonds Alexandre Grothendieck, transcriptions: the TEI customisation the export uses</title>
        <author>Commutator</author>
      </titleStmt>
      <publicationStmt>
        <publisher>Commutator</publisher>
        <availability status="free">
          <p>This file is the maintainers' own work, under the licence of the
             repository's code (see LICENSE). It describes the encoding of
             transcriptions of a fonds that is under rights — see RIGHTS.md —
             and reproduces none of it.</p>
        </availability>
        <ref target="https://grothendieck.commutator.io/tei/grothendieck.odd">https://grothendieck.commutator.io/tei/grothendieck.odd</ref>
      </publicationStmt>
      <sourceDesc>
        <p>Written against the export produced by
           <ref target="../scripts/tei.mjs">scripts/tei.mjs</ref> from the
           transcriptions in <ref target="../transcripts/">transcripts/</ref>,
           which are the source of record.</p>
      </sourceDesc>
    </fileDesc>
    <encodingDesc>
      <p>An ODD in the ordinary sense: one document from which both the schema
         and the documentation of this customisation are derived.</p>
    </encodingDesc>
  </teiHeader>

  <text>
    <body>

      <div>
        <head>What this declares</head>

        <p>TEI P5 has some six hundred elements. The export of this corpus uses
           sixty-four, and they are not a sample of the six hundred: they are
           the exact reach of a mechanical conversion from a LaTeX subset of
           eight private macros, a handful of list and heading commands, and
           mathematics carried as TeX. Every element in the body exists because
           a macro maps onto it; every element in the header exists because
           <ref target="../scripts/tei.mjs">scripts/tei.mjs</ref> writes it.
           Nothing in the export is a judgement the <code>.tex</code> did not
           already make.</p>

        <p>The list below is therefore a promise: these elements and no others,
           these attribute values and no others. <ref target="../scripts/tei-validate.mjs">scripts/tei-validate.mjs</ref>
           holds the export to it on every deploy, in both directions. An
           element used and not declared is an error. An element declared and
           used nowhere is an error too, because a declaration nobody reads is
           a claim about the corpus that is not true, and a subset that can
           only grow ends up as the full schema again. A value outside a closed
           list is an error, which keeps <att>type</att> on a
           <gi>div</gi> from turning into a free-text field one batch at a
           time.</p>

        <p>The customisation is a pure subset. It removes elements and closes
           value lists; it adds nothing, renames nothing and changes no content
           model. A file valid against it is therefore also valid against
           unmodified TEI P5, and the validator checks both.</p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <head>What the apparatus distinguishes</head>

        <p>The transcriptions are not diplomatic. They encode the mathematics
           and a critical apparatus, and nothing about the paper. The apparatus
           makes five distinctions, and each has its own element:</p>
        <list>
          <item><gi>gap</gi>: a word read as illegible, never guessed
             (<code>\ill</code>);</item>
          <item><gi>unclear</gi>: a reading offered with doubt
             (<code>\uncertain</code>);</item>
          <item><gi>supplied</gi>: a word restored by the transcriber
             (<code>\supplied</code>), and <gi>add</gi>: a word added by
             Grothendieck himself, between the lines or over a struck word
             (<code>\add</code>);</item>
          <item><gi>del</gi>: a word he struck out (<code>\struck</code>);</item>
          <item><gi>note</gi>: the transcriber's remark
             (<code>\note</code>), or his own marginal note
             (<code>\marginal</code>), which <att>type</att> tells
             apart.</item>
        </list>
        <p>Mathematics is carried as TeX, untouched, in <gi>formula</gi>, and
           commutative diagrams as tikz-cd source in a <gi>formula</gi> inside a
           <gi>figure</gi>. An apparatus mark inside a formula (an illegible
           exponent, a struck symbol) stays inside the TeX, under the same
           macros, where both the PDF and the reading view typeset it.</p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <head>The customisation</head>

        <schemaSpec ident="grothendieck" start="TEI" prefix="tei_"
                    source="https://www.tei-c.org/Vault/P5/4.12.0/xml/tei/odd/p5subset.xml">

          <!-- Infrastructure: the classes and macros every schema needs. It
               declares no element this export emits. -->
          <moduleRef key="tei"/>

          <!-- The document and its divisions: one division per batch, and a
               division for each \section and \subsection inside it. -->
          <moduleRef key="textstructure" include="TEI body div text"/>

          <!-- The reading: text, lists, headings, page breaks, and the
               apparatus. -->
          <moduleRef key="core"
                     include="add author bibl date del gap head hi item label list name note p pb
                              pubPlace publisher quote ref resp respStmt title unclear"/>

          <!-- Provenance: which model read the pages and when, every revision
               since, the export, and the legal status. -->
          <moduleRef key="header"
                     include="appInfo application availability change edition editionStmt
                              editorialDecl encodingDesc fileDesc idno langUsage language
                              profileDesc projectDesc publicationStmt revisionDesc schemaRef
                              sourceDesc teiHeader titleStmt"/>

          <!-- The folder at Montpellier, as the university identifies it, and
               its facsimile. -->
          <moduleRef key="msdescription"
                     include="additional collection history msContents msDesc msIdentifier
                              origDate origin repository summary surrogates"/>
          <moduleRef key="namesdates" include="country orgName settlement"/>

          <!-- Mathematics, and the diagrams that hold it. -->
          <moduleRef key="figures" include="figure formula"/>

          <!-- The transcriber's restorations. -->
          <moduleRef key="transcr" include="supplied"/>

          <!-- The closed value lists. They are the part a consumer can rely
               on: a value here is drawn from a list, not invented per file,
               and the validator refuses anything outside it. -->

          <elementSpec ident="div" module="textstructure" mode="change">
            <attList>
              <attDef ident="type" mode="change" usage="opt">
                <desc>What the division is. Absent on the one division a
                   batch opens when a <code>\subsection</code> comes before
                   any <code>\section</code>: that division stands in for
                   the section the transcription does not name.</desc>
                <valList type="closed" mode="replace">
                  <valItem ident="batch">
                    <desc>The whole batch: twenty pages or fewer of one folder,
                       the unit a transcription covers.</desc>
                  </valItem>
                  <valItem ident="section">
                    <desc>A heading of the transcription
                       (<code>\section</code>): his own title where the page
                       has one, the edition's where it does not, and the
                       <gi>head</gi> or an editorial note says which.</desc>
                  </valItem>
                  <valItem ident="subsection">
                    <desc>A heading one level down
                       (<code>\subsection</code>).</desc>
                  </valItem>
                </valList>
              </attDef>
            </attList>
          </elementSpec>

          <elementSpec ident="figure" module="figures" mode="change">
            <attList>
              <attDef ident="type" mode="change" usage="req">
                <valList type="closed" mode="replace">
                  <valItem ident="diagram">
                    <desc>A commutative diagram, carried as tikz-cd source in
                       the <gi>formula</gi> it contains. The only figure the
                       export has: the transcription reproduces no drawing.</desc>
                  </valItem>
                </valList>
              </attDef>
            </attList>
          </elementSpec>

          <elementSpec ident="formula" module="figures" mode="change">
            <attList>
              <attDef ident="notation" mode="change" usage="req">
                <valList type="closed" mode="replace">
                  <valItem ident="TeX">
                    <desc>LaTeX mathematics, as written in the source, under
                       the macros of the repository's preamble.</desc>
                  </valItem>
                  <valItem ident="tikz-cd">
                    <desc>A <code>tikzcd</code> environment, whole.</desc>
                  </valItem>
                </valList>
              </attDef>
              <attDef ident="rend" mode="change" usage="opt">
                <valList type="closed" mode="replace">
                  <valItem ident="display">
                    <desc>Set apart on its own line. Absent on inline
                       formulas.</desc>
                  </valItem>
                </valList>
              </attDef>
            </attList>
          </elementSpec>

          <elementSpec ident="gap" module="core" mode="change">
            <attList>
              <attDef ident="reason" mode="change" usage="req">
                <valList type="closed" mode="replace">
                  <valItem ident="illegible">
                    <desc>Looked at and not read. The transcription never
                       guesses under this mark; a guess is
                       <gi>unclear</gi>.</desc>
                  </valItem>
                </valList>
              </attDef>
            </attList>
          </elementSpec>

          <elementSpec ident="note" module="core" mode="change">
            <attList>
              <attDef ident="type" mode="change" usage="opt">
                <desc>Whose note it is. The distinction is the point: an
                   <val>authorial</val> note is writing on the leaf, an
                   <val>editorial</val> one is not. Present on every note in
                   the text; absent only on the two notes the header writes
                   itself, on the inventory's dating and on the facsimile's
                   cover sheet, which belong to the edition and not to the
                   apparatus.</desc>
                <valList type="closed" mode="replace">
                  <valItem ident="editorial">
                    <desc>The transcriber's remark, always with
                       <att>resp</att>.</desc>
                  </valItem>
                  <valItem ident="authorial">
                    <desc>Grothendieck's own note in the margin, always with
                       <att>place</att>.</desc>
                  </valItem>
                </valList>
              </attDef>
              <attDef ident="place" mode="change" usage="opt">
                <valList type="closed" mode="replace">
                  <valItem ident="margin"/>
                </valList>
              </attDef>
            </attList>
          </elementSpec>

          <elementSpec ident="hi" module="core" mode="change">
            <attList>
              <attDef ident="rend" mode="change" usage="req">
                <desc>The typography of the transcription, which carries his
                   own emphasis where the page has it.</desc>
                <valList type="closed" mode="replace">
                  <valItem ident="italic"/>
                  <valItem ident="bold"/>
                  <valItem ident="monospace"/>
                  <valItem ident="sup"/>
                </valList>
              </attDef>
            </attList>
          </elementSpec>

          <elementSpec ident="list" module="core" mode="change">
            <attList>
              <attDef ident="rend" mode="change" usage="req">
                <desc>The LaTeX environment the list came from. His own
                   numbering, where a list carries one, is a <gi>label</gi>
                   before the item and not implied by this value.</desc>
                <valList type="closed" mode="replace">
                  <valItem ident="itemize"/>
                  <valItem ident="enumerate"/>
                </valList>
              </attDef>
            </attList>
          </elementSpec>

          <elementSpec ident="idno" module="header" mode="change">
            <attList>
              <attDef ident="type" mode="change" usage="req">
                <valList type="closed" mode="replace">
                  <valItem ident="cote">
                    <desc>The folder's shelfmark in the inventory of the
                       Université de Montpellier.</desc>
                  </valItem>
                </valList>
              </attDef>
            </attList>
          </elementSpec>

          <elementSpec ident="name" module="core" mode="change">
            <attList>
              <attDef ident="type" mode="change" usage="req">
                <valList type="closed" mode="replace">
                  <valItem ident="model">
                    <desc>A language model that read the pages, named as the
                       transcription's header names it, with its identifier.
                       The only person-like agent the export names: no human
                       has checked a transcription against the pages.</desc>
                  </valItem>
                </valList>
              </attDef>
            </attList>
          </elementSpec>

          <elementSpec ident="schemaRef" module="header" mode="change">
            <attList>
              <attDef ident="type" mode="change" usage="req">
                <valList type="closed" mode="replace">
                  <valItem ident="ODD">
                    <desc>This file, at its published URL.</desc>
                  </valItem>
                </valList>
              </attDef>
            </attList>
          </elementSpec>

        </schemaSpec>
      </div>

      <div>
        <head>How the schema is derived, and how the export is held to it</head>

        <p>This ODD is the source. The RELAX NG schema is derived from it on
           every deploy and is not kept in the repository, for the reason no
           other derived file is kept: a copy that can drift from its source
           will. It is published beside this file, at
           <ref target="https://grothendieck.commutator.io/tei/grothendieck.rng">https://grothendieck.commutator.io/tei/grothendieck.rng</ref>,
           and every exported file names it in an <code>xml-model</code>
           processing instruction, so an XML editor that opens a transcription
           validates it against this customisation without being told to.</p>

        <p>The derivation uses the Consortium's own stylesheets,
           <code>odd2odd.xsl</code> then <code>odd2relax.xsl</code>, from TEI
           Stylesheets 7.61.0 on Saxon-HE 12.5, against the
           <code>p5subset.xml</code> of TEI P5 4.12.0 named in the
           <att>source</att> of the <gi>schemaSpec</gi> above. All four are
           fetched at pinned versions and checked against SHA-256 digests
           recorded in <ref target="../scripts/tei-validate.mjs">scripts/tei-validate.mjs</ref>,
           which runs <code>npm run tei:validate</code>:</p>
        <list>
          <item>this ODD, against unmodified <code>tei_all.rng</code> of
             TEI P5 4.12.0, with jing;</item>
          <item>every exported file, against the same <code>tei_all.rng</code>
             and against the schema derived from this ODD, with jing;</item>
          <item>the export against this file directly: every element used is
             declared, every element declared is used, and every value of an
             attribute with a closed list is on it.</item>
        </list>
        <p>Any failure fails the deploy. The Schematron constraints that
           <code>tei_all</code> embeds are not run over the corpus; the ones
           that bear on this subset are the pointer checks, and the export
           writes every pointer it uses (<code>#pass</code>, <code>#ed</code>
           and one per revising model) together with its target.</p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <head>What this rests on</head>
        <listBibl>
          <bibl><author>TEI Consortium</author>. <title level="m">TEI P5:
             Guidelines for Electronic Text Encoding and Interchange</title>,
             version 4.12.0, 28 July 2026.
             <ref target="https://www.tei-c.org/Vault/P5/4.12.0/">www.tei-c.org/Vault/P5/4.12.0/</ref>.
             The version the corpus is validated against, by the Vault URL
             rather than the moving one.</bibl>
          <bibl><author>Rahtz, Sebastian</author> and
             <author>Burnard, Lou</author>. <title level="a">Reviewing the TEI
             ODD System</title>. In <title level="m">DocEng '13: Proceedings of
             the 2013 ACM Symposium on Document Engineering</title>,
             pp. 193–196. New York: ACM, 2013.
             <ref target="https://doi.org/10.1145/2494266.2494321">doi:10.1145/2494266.2494321</ref>.</bibl>
          <bibl>The customisation of the Hopper ledgers,
             <ref target="https://hopper.commutator.io/tei/hopper.odd">hopper.commutator.io/tei/hopper.odd</ref>,
             on which this one is modelled.</bibl>
        </listBibl>
      </div>

    </body>
  </text>
</TEI>
